Looking at the diff: in `parseYamlInteger$1` (used by `intJsonTag`, i.e. `JSON_SCHEMA`), the sign-stripping condition was changed from `value[0] === "-" || value[0] === "+"` to `value[0] === "" || value[0] === "+"`.

`value[0]` is never the empty string — it's either a single character or `undefined` for an empty string. So the whole condition is now always false, and the sign/hex-prefix handling is dead code. Consequences for explicitly tagged integers under `JSON_SCHEMA`:

- `"-0x10"` no longer has its sign stripped, so it never takes the `0x` branch and falls through to `parseInt("-0x10", 10)` → `-0` instead of `-16` (same for negative octal/binary).
- `"+0x10"` no longer has its `+` stripped, so it falls through to `parseInt("+0x10", 10)` → `0` instead of `16`.

Decimal strings like `"-5"` happen to still work because `parseInt` handles the sign itself, which is why the core suite stays green (the core int tag uses the unchanged `parseYamlInteger$2`).

